.TH HTTP_OPEN 3
.SH NAME
http_open \- start an HTTP request
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <http.h>
.P
.BI "int http_open(http* " h ", http_req_t " type ", const char* " location ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR http_open ()
starts a request of the method
.I type
(GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, PATCH or HEAD).
.I location
is an URL such as
.B https://api.example.com:8443/v1/models?x=1
or only a path that starts with a slash, which uses the host of the structure.
A scheme of https selects TLS and port 443, anything else port 80, a port after the
host name overrides this. Characters from the first CR, LF or NUL on are ignored so that
a location cannot add headers. An URL without a path asks for "/".
.P
The host is resolved with dns_ip4(3), which blocks, unless
.I h->addr
has an address already (it keeps the address of an earlier request, so a structure should
not be used for two different hosts). The request and an empty response are created and
put on top of
.I h->request
and
.IR h->response ,
the socket is created by
.BR http_socket (3),
the connection is started and the socket is registered with
.BR io_onlywantwrite (3).
Nothing is sent yet: the request is written by
.BR http_canwrite (3)
when the connection is established, so headers and a body can be added with
.BR http_header (3),
.BR http_body (3)
and
.BR http_chunk (3)
in between.
.SH RETURN VALUE
1 if the connection is on its way (also if connect(2) is still in progress), 0 on failure; a name that cannot be resolved is reported with errmsg(3).
.SH SEE ALSO
http(3), http_get(3), http_post(3), http_header(3), http_canwrite(3)
